Negative control for the never-gate-the-pool law (T2.4 falsifiability, `AC1.2` ·
`P9` · `parity.yaml` A1 `never_gates` · `ESC5`).

Lets the PAGE matrix reach the GLOBAL session pool, and reaches it where the
pool is DRAWN. Two hunks, one defect: registering the scenario's declared matrix
also narrows `availableScopes` — the pool's own scope registry — to the actors
that matrix resolves a context for, and `addableScopes` (which the
`SessionSwitcher` renders its "add a session" controls from) starts honouring
that page-level registry alongside the app-level allow-list. On the client-emails page the
matrix resolves `CLIENT` alone, so opening a client-emails page silently removes
the way to add a STAFF session from the pool.

It reads like a tidying-up ("the page knows which actors it supports, so tell the
switcher too") and is precisely the defect the design forbids: scope is global
app chrome (`G9`), the greying belongs to the acting-for picker's actor rows and
nowhere else, and the client-emails page itself ships a playable staff track while its matrix
marks `STAFF` as `never` (`ESC5`) — so a matrix-driven pool would contradict the
feature that is the oracle. The second hunk is the whole point of the re-author:
the first version stopped at the registry ref, which nothing the switcher renders
reads, so the planted defect had no observable and the control proved nothing.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds: the parity cases of
`app/components/scope/__tests__/acting-for-segment.spec.ts` — the pool is
unaffected while the actor row is greyed. The observable the developer built
against: after the segment registers the page's matrix, the pool's own scope
registry is unchanged AND the switcher still offers all four ways in
(`actor-scope-add-{account,client,staff,guest}`), with a staff session still
switchable.

